					<description><![CDATA[<p><img width="406" height="232" src="https://www.omgubuntu.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2025/05/nordvpn-406x232.jpg" class="attachment-post-list size-post-list wp-post-image" alt="NordVPN logo overlaid on the Linux penguin mascot." decoding="async" srcset="https://www.omgubuntu.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2025/05/nordvpn-406x232.jpg 406w, https://www.omgubuntu.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2025/05/nordvpn-350x200.jpg 350w, https://www.omgubuntu.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2025/05/nordvpn-840x480.jpg 840w" sizes="(max-width: 406px) 100vw, 406px" />NordVPN are updating their Linux app to offer a &#8220;cleaner, simpler way to manage VPN connections&#8221; from the command line. The NordVPN 6.0 update, which is coming soon (so not out yet), &#8220;cuts down&#8221; the number of commands that &#8220;overlap&#8221;. The company say this will make it easier for users to enable and configure VPN settings. Among the changes, NordVPN 6.0 combines protocol and technology settings in a nordvpn set protocol command, while obfuscation is now handled by NordWhisper, rather than a separate setting. And now that standard servers support P2P traffic, there&#8217;s no longer any need to create a [&#8230;]</p>
<p>You're reading <a href="https://www.omgubuntu.co.uk/2026/10/nordvpn-6-0-linux-cli-changes">NordVPN 6.0 for Linux will feature &#8216;breaking changes&#8217;</a>, a blog post from <a href="https://www.omgubuntu.co.uk">OMG! Ubuntu</a>. Do not reproduce elsewhere without permission.</p>
]]></description>
		
