					<description><![CDATA[<p><img width="406" height="232" src="https://www.omgubuntu.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/tiling-shell-bw-406x232.jpg" class="attachment-post-list size-post-list wp-post-image" alt="" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="https://www.omgubuntu.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/tiling-shell-bw-406x232.jpg 406w, https://www.omgubuntu.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/tiling-shell-bw-300x172.jpg 300w, https://www.omgubuntu.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/tiling-shell-bw-840x480.jpg 840w, https://www.omgubuntu.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/tiling-shell-bw-350x200.jpg 350w, https://www.omgubuntu.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/tiling-shell-bw-768x440.jpg 768w, https://www.omgubuntu.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/tiling-shell-bw.jpg 1440w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 406px) 100vw, 406px" />A new version of the Tiling Shell GNOME extension is out, adding support for GNOME 51 and a faster way to tile app windows using nothing but your mouse. Tiling Shell v18.0 lets you use your right mouse button for tiling system activation. It&#8217;s feature fans of Tiling Shell had requested, many accustomed to it from Windows&#8217; PowerToys&#8217; FancyZones. You click your left button to grab and drag the window then, with it held, hold the right mouse button to trigger the on-screen tiling layout. Release the left button over a zone and the window snaps to it – no [&#8230;]</p>
<p>You're reading <a href="https://www.omgubuntu.co.uk/2026/10/tiling-shell-right-mouse-button-activation">Tiling Shell now supports mouse button layout activation</a>, a blog post from <a href="https://www.omgubuntu.co.uk">OMG! Ubuntu</a>. Do not reproduce elsewhere without permission.</p>
]]></description>
		
